    "text": "Where does the Heuser’s membrane develop from?",
    "explanation": "Heuser's membrane or exocoelomic membrane develops from flattened cells derived from the hypoblast. \r\nOn day 9-10 of pre-embryonic development, flattened cells from the hypoblast begin to migrate to the abembryonic pole. They form a layer of cells just beneath the cytotrophoblast. This layer is called the Heuser's membrane. Once the blastocyst cavity is fully lined with Heuser's membrane, it is called the exocoelomic cavity. The image below shows the formation of Heusner's membrane.",
