  {
    "id": "ANAT-003-000016-I",
    "subject": "Anatomy",
    "text": "Which of the following structures develops into Wharton’s jelly?",
    "explanation": "The extraembryonic mesoderm of the connecting stalk develops into a gelatinous substance known as Wharton’s jelly. \r\nWhen the tail fold of the embryo is formed, the connecting stalk moves towards the ventral aspect of the embryo. The connecting stalk is now attached only in the region of the umbilical opening. The extraembryonic mesoderm of the connecting stalk becomes Wharton's jelly in the umbilical cord. Wharton's jelly protects the blood vessels in the umbilical cord.",
